WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.220
<v Instructor>So CloudFront has more capability</v>

2
00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:03.780
than just a CDN.

3
00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.660
It is allowing you to run custom code at the edge

4
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.850
to do some customization.

5
00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.640
So the idea is that you want to execute edge functions

6
00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.190
as close as possible to users to make sure

7
00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:17.430
that you're verifying some early logic, some early behavior,

8
00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.290
or normalizing some cache keys.

9
00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.630
So an edge function is a code that you write

10
00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:23.850
and that you attach to your CloudFront distribution,

11
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:25.740
and it will run very, very close to your users

12
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.270
in order to minimize the latency

13
00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.100
of whatever you're trying to do.

14
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.570
It doesn't have any cache behavior.

15
00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.910
It is just here to change the request

16
00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:36.750
and the response sent by the users and back to the users.

17
00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:40.230
Now, CloudFront has two different logic for edge functions.

18
00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.770
It has the CloudFront functions and Lambda@Edge.

19
00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.720
We'll see them both in this lecture.

20
00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.630
So the use cases for customization at the end is to,

21
00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:52.140
at the edge is to manipulate HTTP request and responses.

22
00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.430
Is to implement request filtering

23
00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:54.990
before reaching your application.

24
00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:58.410
Is to verify user authentication and authorization.

25
00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:00.630
Is to also maybe generate HTTP responses

26
00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.010
directly at the edge

27
00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:05.340
or do A/B testing by separating users into two groups,

28
00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.550
or for example, doing bot mitigation at the edge.

29
00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.290
The idea is that using edge functions,

30
00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.570
so either CloudFront functions or Lambda@Edge,

31
00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:14.100
you do not have to manage any servers.

32
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.820
Everything is deployed globally and it is serverless.

33
00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.120
So if we look at a traditional architecture of CloudFront,

34
00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.250
we have our clients talking to our edge locations,

35
00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:26.880
talking to original edge cache, talking to our origin.

36
00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.280
Now, Lambda@Edge functions are going to be deployed

37
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.380
at the regional edge cache level,

38
00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.510
whereas CloudFront functions are going to be deployed

39
00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:34.890
on the edge location.

40
00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:37.440
So as you can see, two different level for deployment

41
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.870
of these functions, and that will impact your choice

42
00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:42.810
between CloudFront functions and Lambda@Edge functions.

43
00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.670
So if we look at CloudFront functions,

44
00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.040
they will interact with the viewer request

45
00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.450
and the viewer response.

46
00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.070
These are the request and response

47
00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.380
that happen between the client and CloudFront.

48
00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.560
It does not modify origin request or origin response.

49
00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.140
So these are lightweight functions returning JavaScript,

50
00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.930
and they're meant to do high scale latency sensitive

51
00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.610
CDN customizations.

52
00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.560
So it is a sub-millisecond startup time,

53
00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.780
so it's very, very quick to start and it can handle

54
00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:09.000
millions of requests per second.

55
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:10.890
So we run at edge locations

56
00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:13.020
and it's process-based isolation.

57
00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.510
So you want to change the viewer request and responses

58
00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:16.343
as I said.

59
00:02:16.343 --> 00:02:17.340
So what is the viewer request?

60
00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.500
Well, it's after CloudFront receives a request

61
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.338
from a viewer, and your response is before CloudFront

62
00:02:22.338 --> 00:02:24.889
forwards the response to the viewer.

63
00:02:24.889 --> 00:02:26.940
So the idea is that it's a native feature of CloudFront.

64
00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:29.670
You manage the code directly from within CloudFront,

65
00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:31.720
and this allows you to do some very quick

66
00:02:32.758 --> 00:02:34.996
time sensitive transformations in CloudFront.

67
00:02:34.996 --> 00:02:35.829
Okay?

68
00:02:35.829 --> 00:02:38.340
The idea is that you cannot call external services

69
00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:39.450
from the CloudFront functions.

70
00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.940
You just want to perform some transformations,

71
00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:44.790
some computations or some normalizations

72
00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.740
in a very, very quick fashion.

73
00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.530
Now, Lambda@Edge is a different mindset.

74
00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:52.290
So these were first what CloudFront was offering

75
00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:53.123
as a feature.

76
00:02:53.123 --> 00:02:53.956
CloudFront functions are new,

77
00:02:53.956 --> 00:02:56.490
but Lambda@Edge has been existing for a long time,

78
00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:58.560
and the idea was this is that you have Lambda functions

79
00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.890
that are in either NodeJS or Python,

80
00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:04.740
and you can scale to thousands of requests per second.

81
00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:09.720
So less scale than your CloudFront functions for sure.

82
00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:12.150
And they run on the nearest regional edge cache.

83
00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.130
They're VM-based isolation,

84
00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:15.720
and they're used to change both

85
00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.180
CloudFront requests and responses.

86
00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.910
So we can change the viewer request, the origin request,

87
00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:24.510
which is the request that CloudFront sends to your origin.

88
00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:26.970
You can change the origin response, so the response

89
00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.400
that CloudFront receives from the origin

90
00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.320
and as well as the viewer response.

91
00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:32.160
Okay?

92
00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:33.450
Now, when you want to deploy them,

93
00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.070
that edge functions on CloudFront,

94
00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:36.870
you need to author your functions in one region,

95
00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.220
which is US East one,

96
00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.710
and then CloudFront will replicate this function

97
00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:43.410
to all its regional edge caches.

98
00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.060
Now, if we have a look at

99
00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.770
how these things can be used together.

100
00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.710
So we have edge locations and regional edge caches,

101
00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:52.110
and so the view request goes all the way like this,

102
00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:53.490
then it goes through the origin,

103
00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.650
and then the origin response goes all the way like this

104
00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:57.000
to the viewer response.

105
00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.710
Now your CloudFront functions

106
00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:02.160
are going to run on the edge locations at the viewer request

107
00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:03.690
and viewer response level,

108
00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:06.300
whereas your Lambda@Edge functions are going to run

109
00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.670
at the origin request and origin response level.

110
00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:11.250
This is if you want to use both CloudFront functions

111
00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.620
and Lambda@Edge functions together.

112
00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:14.970
Okay, so this is one option

113
00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:18.090
and we can see a clear separation of duties.

114
00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:22.230
But if you want to just have Lambda@Edge functions,

115
00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:23.700
then in that case, what's going to happen

116
00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:26.160
is that the Lambda@Edge functions are going to operate

117
00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.050
on both the viewer requests response

118
00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:29.790
and the origin request and response,

119
00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.040
but only at the regional edge cache level.

120
00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.290
In this case, we cannot use CloudFront functions

121
00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:36.960
because we cannot use both Lambda@Edge

122
00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:37.830
and CloudFront functions

123
00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:40.560
on the viewer request and responses.

124
00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.110
So this is when you need capabilities of Lambda@Edge

125
00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:44.910
that are not available with CloudFront functions.

126
00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.460
For example, if you need a longer execution time,

127
00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:51.660
network access, third party API calls, these kind of things.

128
00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:54.600
So if we want to compare, you know, on the table,

129
00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.790
the CloudFront functions and Lambda@Edge,

130
00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:58.230
while the runtime support is different,

131
00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:00.660
so one is JavaScript, pure JavaScript code,

132
00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:02.790
the other one is NodeJS and Python,

133
00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:05.280
where it gets executed is on edge locations

134
00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.620
for CloudFront functions, whereas it's original edge cache

135
00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:09.060
is for Lambda@Edge.

136
00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:11.580
The CloudFront triggers is going to be the view request

137
00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:12.840
and response for functions

138
00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.180
and all of them, for them that edge.

139
00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.760
The isolation is process-based versus VM-based.

140
00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:19.170
Now, the max execution time

141
00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:21.210
is what I think is the most important thing.

142
00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:22.920
You only get one millisecond

143
00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:24.450
to execute your CloudFront functions

144
00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:26.160
and therefore they must be very, very quick

145
00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:27.540
and very, very simple.

146
00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:31.470
Whereas Lambda@Edge, you get five seconds of runtime

147
00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:34.080
for viewers triggered and 30 seconds of runtime

148
00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.580
for origin triggers.

149
00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.110
The memory of your CloudFront functions

150
00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:38.850
is low, two megabytes.

151
00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:41.340
Whereas for Lambda@Edge, you have more memory,

152
00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.530
128 megabytes or 10 gigabytes.

153
00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:45.330
The total package size is very, very small

154
00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:46.890
for CloudFront functions, 10 kilobytes.

155
00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:49.050
Whereas Lambda@Edge has more space.

156
00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:51.510
There's no network access, no file system access

157
00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:52.740
for CloudFront functions,

158
00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:54.960
whereas you have some for Lambda@Edge.

159
00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.420
You do not have access to the request body

160
00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:58.470
in CloudFront functions,

161
00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:00.930
you have access to it in Lambda@Edge.

162
00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:02.940
So CloudFront functions is really about

163
00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:04.500
reading headers and so on.

164
00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:06.660
And the pricing is that there's a free tier

165
00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:07.590
for CloudFront functions,

166
00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:09.930
and it's 1/6 of the price of @Edge.

167
00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:12.712
So if you can move your logic from Lambda@Edge

168
00:06:12.712 --> 00:06:14.340
to CloudFront functions, you're better off.

169
00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:15.930
And Lambda@Edge has no free tier

170
00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.630
and you get charged per request and duration.

171
00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:20.640
So when do you use a CloudFront function

172
00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.620
versus a Lambda@Edge function?

173
00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:24.120
Well, CloudFront functions are going to be used

174
00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:25.620
for cache key normalization.

175
00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:27.660
So you want to transform the request attributes

176
00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:30.750
such as the headers, cookies, the query string, the URL

177
00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:32.700
to create an optimal cache key.

178
00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:34.080
You also want to manipulate your headers,

179
00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:34.913
so you want to change them.

180
00:06:34.913 --> 00:06:37.530
So you want to insert, modify, or delete HTTP headers

181
00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:39.270
in the request or response,

182
00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:41.850
or you want to do your rewrites or redirects

183
00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:43.410
or to request authentication

184
00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:45.330
and authorization directly at the edge.

185
00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:46.860
So in which you can create

186
00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.220
and validate user generated tokens, for example, JWT,

187
00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:54.090
to allow or deny request very, very close to your users.

188
00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:56.160
Whereas Lambda@Edge is going to be giving you

189
00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:57.990
a much longer execution time, okay?

190
00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:00.030
Up to five seconds or 30 seconds.

191
00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:01.860
You get adjustable CPU or memory.

192
00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:04.080
You get to have code that can depend on

193
00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:04.950
third party libraries.

194
00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:06.720
For example, you can use the SDK

195
00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.850
to access other AWS services.

196
00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:11.160
You can use the network access to use external services

197
00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:13.920
for processing, and you have file system access

198
00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:15.810
if you wanted to cache some stuff locally,

199
00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:18.780
or you can even access the body of the HTTP request

200
00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:20.430
to manipulate the body itself.

201
00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:23.850
So hopefully the use cases are clear and well-defined.

202
00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:25.500
Now, if you wanted to do authorization

203
00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:27.390
and authentication with CloudFront functions,

204
00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:29.100
the idea is that at the Edge location

205
00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:30.930
you would intercept the content

206
00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:34.080
and you would check the token, the digital JWT token,

207
00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:36.240
which lives in your headers, and then you can check

208
00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:37.860
whether or not it is correct.

209
00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:38.940
And then if it's not correct,

210
00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:41.160
then the client will get an error message directly

211
00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:44.610
without having your request go all the way to your origin.

212
00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:46.350
Whereas your Lambda@Edge function

213
00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.360
will operate on the regional edge cache level.

214
00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:50.310
It could be intercepted by a Lambda function,

215
00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:51.570
which can do some custom logic

216
00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:54.030
and even issue third party API calls

217
00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:56.100
to check whether or not the authorization

218
00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.920
and authentication is properly well set up.

219
00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:01.530
Another very common use case for CloudFront functions

220
00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:04.350
is to load content based on the user agents.

221
00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:05.430
We have an S3 bucket

222
00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:07.680
and Amazon CloudFront with original Edge cache

223
00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:10.080
and we have users accessing it from desktop

224
00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:12.180
and others just accessing it from mobile.

225
00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:14.340
Now they both wanna access a cat picture,

226
00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:16.680
so they do a request for cat picture,

227
00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:17.970
and the resolution is super high.

228
00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:19.380
So 1920-1080.

229
00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:25.440
So it's 1080p and they request it from CloudFront.

230
00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:27.060
So if the request goes well,

231
00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:29.030
well the request is passed all the way through

232
00:08:29.030 --> 00:08:32.520
to the S3 bucket to get that specific image.

233
00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:35.250
But imagine that the same request is done

234
00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:37.950
from our mobile device, and this time the user agent

235
00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:41.760
is not Chrome, it is the iPhone OS Safari.

236
00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:45.420
Now what we can do is instead of sending this big image

237
00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:48.390
back to our mobile, which may be very inefficient,

238
00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:50.370
we can have a CloudFront function

239
00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:53.610
that will inspect the user agent HTTP header

240
00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:56.640
and then redirect based on the device type.

241
00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:58.110
And so there, therefore,

242
00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:00.690
because our device is a mobile device,

243
00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:03.360
then the image is going to be asked into our S3 bucket

244
00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:06.900
is going to be cat 640-320,

245
00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:10.230
which is a lower resolution for our cat picture.

246
00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:11.850
And this is the picture that will be returned

247
00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:14.550
by our S3 bucket into our mobile device.

248
00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:17.550
So the function was able to modify the request

249
00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:20.463
to change the target of these requests.

250
00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:23.130
So the other way you can use Lambda@Edge

251
00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:24.960
is for a global application, for example,

252
00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:27.810
your client gets the website from an S3 bucket,

253
00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:29.400
and then for dynamic API request,

254
00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:32.430
it sends it into CloudFront to leverage the cache.

255
00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:34.740
But your Lambda function can also be used, for example,

256
00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.870
to query data into DynamoDB table,

257
00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:39.600
which gives you a fully global

258
00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:44.040
and serverless way to do an API in AWS.

259
00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:44.873
So that's it.

260
00:09:44.873 --> 00:09:47.160
At the exam level what you need to know

261
00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:49.800
is understand when you need to use Lambda@Edge

262
00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:52.110
or CloudFront functions, and that should be good to go.

263
00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:53.370
But I want to give you a bit more detail

264
00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:55.710
so you can really understand the use cases behind it.

